# Paths that LOOK like secrets by name but are legitimately tracked.  (T-0659)
#
# Read by .githooks/lib/commit-policy.sh from HEAD — an edit here has no effect
# until it is COMMITTED. That is deliberate: otherwise the same `git add -A`
# that sweeps up a secret would also sweep up the line permitting it, and the
# gate would be consulting the attacker's own copy of its policy.
#
# EXACT repo-relative paths only, one per line. No globs — an exception has to
# be a named file someone chose, not a pattern that quietly widens over time.
# Adding a line is a reviewed act: it shows up in the diff as a policy change.
#
# An absent or empty file is NOT an error here and must never become one: this
# list names what may PASS, so having none is the strictest policy there is.
# That is the opposite of allowed-identities, which IS the policy (T-0662).
#
# MEASURED (2026-08-15). Exactly four paths in the WHOLE history of this repo
# match the secret-like predicate. Three are below; each is ordinary source with
# no literal value in it — checked, not assumed.
#
# `.env.example` was DELIBERATELY ABSENT from this list until 2026-08-15. Its
# TG_BOT_TOKEN line held the LIVE bot token — byte for byte the same value as
# /home/www/bot-squad/.env and both watchrobot .env files, compared without
# printing it — in history since 7aec85a, 2026-05-10. Listing it then would have
# meant this gate blessing a file that carried a live credential.
#
# T-0663 part 1 replaced that value with a placeholder, so the file is now what
# its name claims and the exception is legitimate. THE LIVE VALUE IS STILL IN
# HISTORY: removing it from HEAD does not remove it from the commits behind, and
# rotating the token at BotFather is T-0663 part 2 — the stakeholder's call,
# because the same token serves the prod bot.

.env.example
api/app/secret_crypto.py
worker/bot_squad_worker/secret_crypto.py
worker/tests/test_secret_crypto.py
